s:
Let $a$ be the inverse of $152$ modulo $367$. The definition of the inverse means that $152\cdot a \equiv 1\pmod{367}$. 

First, note that $152 \cdot 2 = 304 \equiv -63 \pmod{367}$. To find the multiplicative inverse:
\begin{align*}
-63 \cdot 6 &= -378 \\
&\equiv -11 \pmod{367}
\end{align*}
Next, calculate the inverse of $-11$:
\begin{align*}
-11 \cdot \text{some integer} &\equiv 1 \pmod{367}
\end{align*}
Try multiples of $-11$:
\begin{align*}
-11 \cdot 34 &\equiv 374 \equiv 7 \pmod{367} \\
7 \cdot (-53) &\equiv -371 \equiv -4 \pmod{367} \\
-4 \cdot (-92) &\equiv 368 \equiv 1 \pmod{367}
\end{align*}
Putting it all together:
\begin{align*}
152 \cdot 2 \cdot 6 \cdot 34 \cdot (-53) \cdot (-92) &\equiv 1 \pmod{367}
\end{align*}
Calculating the whole expression modulus $367$:
\begin{align*}
152 \cdot (2 \cdot 6 \cdot 34 \cdot (-53) \cdot (-92)) &\equiv 1 \pmod{367}
\end{align*}
We find that the integer $a$, which satisfies $152\cdot a \equiv 1 \pmod{367}$, is $$a = 2 \cdot 6 \cdot 34 \cdot 53 \cdot 92 = \boxed{18022496}$$. (Note: The result must be reduced modulo $367$ for the actual answer).

Conclusion and boxed answer:
Thus, compute $18022496 \mod 367$:
$$ 18022496 \div 367 \approx 49096 \text{ remainder } 248 $$

So, the multiplicative inverse of $152$ modulo $367$ is $\boxed{248}$.

To solve the problem, we perform polynomial division: Divide $3x^4 + 16x^3 + 5x^2 - 36x + 58$ by $x^2 + 5x + 3$.
\[
\begin{array}{c|c ccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{3x^2} & +1x & -2 \\
\cline{2-6}
x^2+5x+3 & 3x^4 & +16x^3 & +5x^2 & -36x & +58 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-3x^4} & -15x^3 & -9x^2 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & x^3 & -4x^2 & -36x \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -x^3 & -5x^2 & -3x \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & x^2 & -33x & +58 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -x^2 & -5x & -3 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & -28x & +55 \\
\end{array}
\]
Since the degree of $-28x + 55$ is less than the degree of the divisor $x^2 + 5x + 3$, we cannot divide further. Thus, the remainder is $\boxed{-28x + 55}$.

